text:
I. At the inn were the Khevsurs, and beer was boiling in the cauldron;  
they sat on the roof and feasted, and drank from goblets there;  
some played the panduri, and sang over it loudly,  
setting before the listeners heroic tales as a bridge;  
with fervor they counted the names of heroes in verses, sacredly;  
the old men puffed their pipes, the smoke around them like mist,  
they explained their stories, speaking of the heroes’ valor.  
For the instruction of the young they recalled with praise:  
“You know, from now on whoever proves himself a man!”

The captive learned the wisdom of the devils from beyond;  
the sky, the earth, and the forest began to converse with him.  
Only evil could not take root in his heart;  
all the rest of the devilish tricks he learned as skill.  
He fears nothing now, even if lightning were to strike him down.  
The devils grow angry when they see it — the peasant has become enlightened.  
Captivity no longer frightens him, nor do the devils’ schemes;  
today or tomorrow, he hopes, he will give the devils a kicking.

They said this, and at that very moment turned back, ran away like thieves,  
they have drawn swords, they hold spears in their hands;  
it is hard, if we think of it, those moments of life and death; what can they do!  
if they are shamed, it is hard, they will be dressed in robes,  
headscarves will be placed over their heads, and their hats will fall off,  
if they do not exert themselves, like cowards, the wicked.
